In Bitburner, you can capture the return value of a script by using the `run` function with the `--return` flag. Here's an example:
```javascript
// Run a script and capture its return value
const result = run("my-script.js", 1, 2, 3);

// Log the result
console.log(result);
```

The `run` function takes three arguments: the name of the script to run, the number of threads to use, and any arguments to pass to the script. The `--return` flag tells the game to capture the return value of the script and return it as a number.
In the example above, `my-script.js` is the name of the script to run, `1` is the number of threads to use, and `2`, `3` are the arguments to pass to the script. The `result` variable will contain the return value of the script.
Note that the `run` function only works on scripts that are located in the same server as the script that is running it. If you need to run a script on a different server, you will need to use the `scp` function to copy the script to the target server first.

